"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[6544],{46544:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('[{"question":"What\'s the capacitor rating for a Carrier 24ACC636A003?","answer":"The Carrier 24ACC636A003 uses a 45/5 \xb5F dual run capacitor rated at 440V. The 45 \xb5F side powers the compressor and the 5 \xb5F side powers the condenser fan motor. Always replace with the same \xb5F rating — a capacitor within \xb16% is acceptable, but a 440V capacitor can replace a 370V unit safely. Never go below the rated voltage.","citations":[{"docId":"capacitor-sizing-guide","docTitle":"Capacitor Sizing Guide","chunkText":"The higher value runs the compressor; the lower value runs the condenser fan motor. A 440V capacitor can safely replace a 370V in the same circuit. Never install a lower voltage rating than specified.","headingPath":["Common Carrier Unit Ratings","Voltage Ratings"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.97},{"question":"What are the R-410A operating pressures for a 95\xb0F day?","answer":"On a 95\xb0F ambient day with normal operation, expect suction pressure of 118–125 psi and discharge pressure of 390–420 psi for R-410A systems. If suction is below 110 psi, the system is likely low on charge. If discharge exceeds 450 psi, check for overcharge, dirty condenser coil, or a failed condenser fan.","citations":[{"docId":"r410a-refrigerant-safety","docTitle":"R-410A Refrigerant Safety","chunkText":"95\xb0F ambient, normal operation: Suction Pressure 118–125 psi, Discharge Pressure 390–420 psi. Low charge: <110 psi suction. High charge / restriction: >450 psi discharge.","headingPath":["Operating Pressures (typical residential)"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.95},{"question":"How do I know if a hot surface igniter is bad?","answer":"A bad hot surface igniter either won\'t glow at all, or its resistance is out of spec. For silicon carbide igniters, measure 40–70 Ω when cold — replace if above 100 Ω or open circuit. For silicon nitride (more common post-2010), expect 15–50 Ω — replace if above 60 Ω. If 120V is present at the igniter leads but it still won\'t glow, the igniter is open and must be replaced. Handle the new igniter by the harness only — skin oils reduce its lifespan.","citations":[{"docId":"furnace-igniter-replacement","docTitle":"Furnace Igniter Replacement","chunkText":"Silicon carbide: Good Range 40–70 Ω, Replace If >100 Ω or open circuit. Silicon nitride: Good Range 15–50 Ω, Replace If >60 Ω or open circuit.","headingPath":["Resistance Quick Reference"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.96},{"question":"What does a Carrier E3 error code mean?","answer":"A Carrier ComfortLink II E3 code indicates an outdoor unit high pressure fault. Common causes are refrigerant overcharge, a dirty condenser coil blocking airflow, or a failed condenser fan motor. Start by checking that the condenser fan is spinning and the coil is clean. Then check refrigerant pressures — if discharge is above 450 psi on a normal day, you likely have a refrigerant or airflow issue.","citations":[{"docId":"common-ac-error-codes","docTitle":"Common AC Error Codes","chunkText":"E3: Outdoor unit high pressure fault. Likely Cause: Overcharge, dirty coil, failed fan. Action: Check refrigerant charge, clean coil, verify fan operation.","headingPath":["Carrier ComfortLink II Error Codes"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.97},{"question":"How often should I replace the condensate pan tablets?","answer":"Condensate pan tablets (algaecide) should be replaced every 3 months or at each maintenance visit — whichever comes first. They prevent algae and slime buildup that clogs drain lines. Drop one tablet into the drain pan after flushing the drain line. Flush the drain with condensate cleaner or a 50/50 bleach-water solution before adding a fresh tablet.","citations":[{"docId":"air-handler-maintenance","docTitle":"Air Handler Maintenance Guide","chunkText":"Flush annually with condensate cleaner or 50/50 bleach/water. Install condensate pan tablets (algaecide) to prevent buildup.","headingPath":["Condensate System","Drain Line Maintenance"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.91},{"question":"What is the minimum SEER2 rating required in Texas?","answer":"In Texas (Southeast/Southwest region), the minimum SEER2 efficiency required for new AC and heat pump equipment as of January 2023 is 15.2 SEER2. This replaced the old 14 SEER minimum. Note that SEER2 ratings run about 4–7% lower than equivalent SEER due to a more realistic test procedure — a 16 SEER unit is approximately 15.2 SEER2.","citations":[{"docId":"seer2-ratings-explained","docTitle":"SEER2 Ratings Explained","chunkText":"Texas falls in the Southeast/Southwest region — minimum is 15.2 SEER2. SEER2 uses a more realistic test procedure (M1 test method) that accounts for static pressure in ductwork, resulting in ratings approximately 4–7% lower than equivalent SEER equipment.","headingPath":["Minimum Efficiency Requirements (2023+)"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.98},{"question":"How do I check if a compressor has bad valves?","answer":"The quickest test for bad compressor valves is the equalization test: let the system run for 15 minutes, then shut off the compressor by disconnecting the Y wire at the thermostat. Watch the suction pressure gauge — it should hold steady or rise slowly as pressures equalize. If suction pressure shoots up to equal discharge pressure within 60 seconds, the compressor valves have failed and the compressor needs replacement.","citations":[{"docId":"compressor-diagnosis","docTitle":"Compressor Diagnosis","chunkText":"Shut off compressor (disconnect Y wire). Watch suction pressure — should hold or rise slowly. If suction rises to discharge level within 60 seconds: compressor valves are failed.","headingPath":["Mechanical Tests","Compressor Valve Test"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.94},{"question":"What type of UV light kills mold on the evaporator coil?","answer":"UV-C light (ultraviolet-C) is used to kill mold, bacteria, and viruses on the evaporator coil. A coil-mounted UV lamp is positioned to shine directly on the coil surface 24/7 when the system is running. UV-C light at 254nm disrupts the DNA of microorganisms, preventing reproduction. We install single-lamp coil units (SKU: MAINT-UV-SYSTEM) for standard applications. The lamp requires annual replacement to maintain effectiveness.","citations":[{"docId":"indoor-air-quality","docTitle":"Indoor Air Quality (IAQ) Solutions","chunkText":"Ultraviolet-C (UV-C) light kills bacteria, mold spores, and viruses on the evaporator coil and in the airstream. Coil-mounted: Single lamp pointed at evaporator coil — prevents microbial growth on coil surface.","headingPath":["UV Air Purifiers"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.93},{"question":"What does a heat pump reversing valve do?","answer":"The reversing valve switches a heat pump between heating and cooling mode by reversing the direction refrigerant flows through the system. In cooling mode, the outdoor coil acts as the condenser (releasing heat outside) and the indoor coil absorbs heat. Energizing the reversing valve swaps these roles so the outdoor coil absorbs heat from outside air and the indoor coil releases it inside — that\'s heating mode. Carrier and Bryant heat pumps energize the reversing valve in heating mode (B terminal), while most other brands energize it in cooling mode (O terminal).","citations":[{"docId":"thermostat-wiring-guide","docTitle":"Thermostat Wiring Guide","chunkText":"Orange: O — Reversing valve (heat pumps — energized in cooling). Dark Blue: B — Reversing valve (Carrier/Bryant — energized in heating).","headingPath":["Standard Wire Color Codes"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.92},{"question":"What approval is needed for an estimate over $5,000?","answer":"Any estimate with a best-option total over $5,000 requires Owner approval (Maria Lopez) before it can be presented to the customer. The technician or Quill AI can draft the estimate, but the Owner must review and approve it in the system first. This threshold also applies to discounts over 10% and refunds over $100.","citations":[{"docId":"company-policies","docTitle":"Northwind Heating & Air — Company Policies","chunkText":"$5,000+: Owner (Maria Lopez) approval required before presenting to customer. Refunds >$100: Owner approval required. Discounts >10%: Owner approval required.","headingPath":["Approval Thresholds"]}],"notFound":false,"confidence":0.99}]')}}]);